<div>
<h2 id="free-books-this-week">100+ Free Books Are Live Right Now</h2>

<p>Right now, as you read this, a four-day giveaway called <strong>Stuff Your E-Reader</strong> is running through <strong>August 8, 2026</strong>. Hosted by the Indie Author Collective, it is an all-genre book blast with more than <strong>100 free ebooks</strong> from independent authors, spanning fantasy, thriller, romance, mystery, science fiction, young adult, and dozens of other categories. Everything you download is yours to keep forever, and there is no limit on how many books you can grab.</p>

<p>These events are usually called <strong>Stuff Your Kindle Day</strong> in the wider ebook community, even when they are not Kindle-exclusive — and that name matters for search, because it is the phrase thousands of readers type every month when a new event goes live. The August edition is one of the biggest of the year, and it is happening right now.</p>

<p>This guide explains what Stuff Your Kindle Day events actually are, lists every free ebook event on the August 2026 calendar, and — most importantly for readers of this site — shows you how to take those free books and read them as EPUB on <em>any</em> device, not just the one the store page points you toward.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-stuff-your-kindle-day">What Is Stuff Your Kindle Day?</h2>

<p>Stuff Your Kindle Day is a recurring ebook promotion organized by <strong>independent author collectives</strong>, not by Amazon. On a set of dates announced in advance, participating indie authors temporarily set their books to a price of <strong>$0.00</strong> (or $0.99 for some premium titles) in the ebook stores where they sell — most commonly Amazon and Kobo. Readers browse the event hub, click through to the store listings, and "buy" the books for free during the window. When the window closes, the books go back to their normal prices, but anything you already downloaded stays in your library forever.</p>

<p>Several groups run their own recurring versions of the concept:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Indie Author Collective (IAC)</strong> — runs <strong>Stuff Your E-Reader</strong>, a monthly all-genre blast that is the biggest recurring event in the space.</li>
  <li><strong>Indie Author Hive</strong> — runs <strong>Buzzfest</strong>, a similar all-genre event that often overlaps with IAC's dates.</li>
  <li><strong>Book Below</strong> — runs genre-focused collections such as the Mystery and Crime Collection and Fantasy and Sci-Fi Worlds.</li>
  <li><strong>Indie Authors Central</strong> — runs themed events like Stepping Through Time for historical fiction.</li>
</ul>

<p>Because the events recur roughly every month, there is almost always one coming up. In 2026 alone, <a href="https://mashable.com/tech/stuff-your-e-reader-free-books" rel="noopener">Mashable has covered</a> the dark romance and mafia romance giveaway in May, the Summer Solstice fantasy event in July, and the Romance Book Blast on July 28 — and now the August all-genre edition. That recurring cadence is exactly why these events keep generating search interest all year.</p>

<h2 id="august-2026-calendar">August 2026 Free Ebook Event Calendar</h2>

<p>August 2026 is one of the busiest months of the year for free ebook events. Here is the full lineup as of publication:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>Dates (2026)</th><th>Host</th><th>Genres</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Mystery and Crime Collection</strong></td><td>Aug 1–2</td><td>Book Below</td><td>Mystery, crime, suspense, women sleuths, cozies</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Stuff Your E-Reader</strong></td><td><strong>Aug 5–8 (live now)</strong></td><td>Indie Author Collective</td><td>All genres — 30+ categories, 100+ books</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Buzzfest</strong></td><td><strong>Aug 5–8 (live now)</strong></td><td>Indie Author Hive</td><td>All genres — romance, fantasy, horror, mystery, contemporary</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Stepping Through Time</strong></td><td>Aug 12–16</td><td>Indie Authors Central</td><td>Historical fiction, contemporary stories</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Fantasy and Sci-Fi Worlds</strong></td><td>Aug 15–16</td><td>Book Below</td><td>Fantasy, science fiction, paranormal</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The two events running today, <strong>Stuff Your E-Reader</strong> and <strong>Buzzfest</strong>, are the biggest of the month. Both close at the end of August 8, so if you want to stock up, this is the window. The later events in the month give you a second chance to fill any gaps.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-get-free-books">How to Get the Free Books</h2>

<p>The sign-up process is deliberately simple, but it helps to know where to look before the clock runs out:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Open the event hub.</strong> For the current blast, go to the <a href="https://www.indieauthorcollective.com/stuff-your-ereader" rel="noopener">Indie Author Collective's Stuff Your E-Reader page</a>, which has a searchable hub of every participating title.</li>
  <li><strong>Filter the catalog.</strong> The hub lets you filter by genre, by platform (Kindle or Kobo), by price (free or $0.99), and by representation. Start with your favorite genres to avoid decision fatigue.</li>
  <li><strong>Click through to the store.</strong> Each book links to its listing on Amazon or Kobo. During the event window the price shows as $0.00.</li>
  <li><strong>Complete the purchase.</strong> Buy the book at $0.00 exactly as you would any other ebook. It is added to your Amazon or Kobo account permanently.</li>
  <li><strong>Repeat as much as you like.</strong> There is no limit on the number of books you can download during the event, and they never expire or get reclaimed.</li>
</ol>

<p>One important distinction: these giveaways are <strong>not</strong> the same as Kindle Unlimited. Kindle Unlimited is a subscription where you borrow up to 20 books at a time and lose access when you return them or cancel. Stuff Your Kindle Day downloads are outright purchases at a zero price — you keep them forever, and they do not count against any borrowing limit.</p>

<h2 id="kindle-or-kobo">Kindle or Kobo: The Format Question</h2>

<p>Here is where the event intersects with the reason this site exists. The books in these blasts are sold through two main stores, and the two stores hand you <strong>different file formats</strong>:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Amazon Kindle versions</strong> come in Amazon's proprietary AZW3/KFX format. They read perfectly on Kindles, and they can be sent to the Kindle app on phones and tablets, but they do not open on other brands of e-reader.</li>
  <li><strong>Kobo versions</strong> are standard <strong>EPUB</strong> files. Kobo's whole ecosystem — its readers, its apps, and its store — is built around EPUB, so a Kobo purchase is a portable, open-format file from the start.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you are an EPUB-first reader, the smart play during these events is to prefer the <strong>Kobo platform filter</strong> on the event hub. You get the same free book, but in a format you can read on a Kobo, a PocketBook, a Boox, a phone, a tablet, or a computer — now and in ten years. If a title is only offered on Amazon, you can still grab it and convert it afterward; see the workflow below.</p>

<p>We compared the two platforms in depth in <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook 2026</a>, but the short version for free-book events is: Kindle maximizes convenience inside Amazon's walled garden, while Kobo maximizes long-term ownership of an open file.</p>

<h2 id="read-on-any-device">How to Read Your Free Books as EPUB on Any Device</h2>

<p>Free books are only useful if you can actually read them where you want. Here is the complete workflow, from store download to any device in your house:</p>

<h3 id="step-1-download">Step 1: Download the files</h3>

<p>On Amazon, open your Content Library, find the book, and use the download option to save it to your computer (Amazon now offers DRM-free EPUB and PDF downloads for many titles, which we covered when the feature launched). On Kobo, download the EPUB from your library page in the Kobo desktop app or the web account area.</p>

<h3 id="step-2-convert">Step 2: Convert to the format your device needs</h3>

<p>If you bought a Kindle-format book and want to read it on a Kobo, a PocketBook, or any other EPUB-native device, you need to convert it. The free, open-source standard for this is <strong>Calibre</strong>:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Install Calibre and add the downloaded file to your library (drag and drop works).</li>
  <li>Select the book and choose <em>Convert books</em>.</li>
  <li>Pick <strong>EPUB</strong> as the output format for Kobo, PocketBook, Boox, or phone apps.</li>
  <li>Click OK. Calibre rebuilds the file as a clean, reflowable EPUB.</li>
</ol>

<p>We walk through the full process, including the settings that produce the best results, in <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Calibre to Kindle: the EPUB workflow</a> and <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">how to send EPUB files to a Kindle</a>.</p>

<h3 id="step-3-organize">Step 3: Organize everything in one library</h3>

<p>Before you load books onto devices, add them all to a single Calibre library. That gives you a permanent backup, automatic metadata and cover fetching, and a searchable catalog of every free book you have collected. For a full walkthrough of tags, series, and wireless syncing, see our <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre library management guide</a>. If you read on a Kobo, the <a href="/blog/calibre-9-8-kobo-kepub-sync/">KEPUB sync workflow</a> will also keep your reading position and annotations in sync.</p>

<h3 id="step-4-load">Step 4: Load the books onto your device</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Device</th><th>How to load free books</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Kindle</strong></td><td>Use Send to Kindle with the EPUB file, or plug in over USB and copy the file to the documents folder.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Kobo</strong></td><td>Drag the EPUB onto the device over USB, or use Calibre's Kobo driver for automatic conversion and sync.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>PocketBook / Boox / Android</strong></td><td>Copy the EPUB to the device storage, or sync through Calibre's content server.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Phone / tablet</strong></td><td>Open the EPUB in any capable reader app; our roundup of the <a href="/blog/best-epub-reader-apps-2026/">best EPUB reader apps</a> covers the top choices.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="keep-forever">Keep Your Free Library Forever</h2>

<p>The "yours to keep forever" promise is the whole appeal of these events, but a digital library only stays yours if you take a few precautions:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Back everything up.</strong> Store the original files in Calibre and in a cloud folder. Store accounts can change, but files on your disk are permanent.</li>
  <li><strong>Prefer DRM-free copies.</strong> Most indie authors in these events distribute DRM-free by default, which is exactly why the books work so well across devices. If a listing mentions DRM, weigh whether the convenience of the free price is worth the lock-in. We explain the broader ecosystem in our <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB library guide</a> and our roundup of <a href="/blog/amazon-drm-free-kindle-books-2026/">DRM-free Kindle books</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose EPUB as your archival format.</strong> EPUB is an open standard that every major reader supports. A library of EPUB files will outlive any single store, device, or promotion — which is precisely why our free <a href="/">PDF to EPUB converter</a> exists.</li>
</ul>

<p>Done right, a year of Stuff Your Kindle Day events can build you a library of hundreds of books — many of them surprisingly good — for exactly zero dollars, readable on any screen you own.</p>

